94:## Decisions
95-- **The health tracker is generic** (`sensor_health`), not light-only, so the soil process can reuse it. It is a small public struct with no malloc.
96-- **Health input:** any non-`GT_OK` result from `gt_light_read` counts as one failure. The driver's 3 retries are already inside a single failed read.
97-- **Health output:** `gt_health_record` returns a transition event (`NONE` / `OFFLINE` / `ONLINE`) and the caller logs it. The tracker does no I/O.
98-- **Time is passed in** as `now_ms`, matching `gt_sampler_tick`, so tests need no clock.
99-- **Test ID prefixes:** `SHR` (sensor health record), `SHS` (sensor health should-read), `SHE` (sensor health event name), `LGF` (log format), `MSE` (message encode), `MSD` (message decode), `CRC` (CRC-32), `RCE` (record encode), `RCD` (record decode), `IFD` (IPC frame decode), `ICO` (IPC client open), `ICC` (IPC client close), `ICS` (IPC client send), `ICR` (IPC client reconnect), `IOV` (IPC client overflow), `IPL` (IPC loopback over a real socket).
100-- **Log ownership:** the light sensor's events (`i2c_read_error`, `sensor_offline`, `sensor_online`) are Lily's, as the light process's read-failure handling. The shared formatter (`gt_log_format`) has no owner in the Ownership map because every component uses it, so it counts as **Joint → Architecture integration**. Lily writes it first, as its first caller; give Lucas a heads-up so his components reuse it.
101-- **Log line:** `<timestamp_us> <LEVEL> <component> <event>[ key=value ...]`. The timestamp is µs since the Unix epoch, as a plain integer, the same unit as the on-disk record, so logs and records can be joined. The levels are `DEBUG` / `INFO` / `WARN` / `ERROR`. The light process logs `sensor_offline` as WARN and `sensor_online` as INFO.
102-- **Log sink deferred:** the formatter only fills a caller-provided buffer. Where lines go (stderr, a file or the monitor process) and reading the wall clock are decided with the light process. DESIGN.md doesn't specify either.
103-- **Hop 2 (hub→storage) carries the 24 B on-disk record** (decided 2026-10-23). The hub stamps `seq` and the µs timestamp, builds the record (CRC included), and storage checks it with `gt_record_decode`, then appends it unchanged. There are two reasons. First, DESIGN.md already has the hub stamping the time and storage reading *records* from `storage.sock`. Second, a record that waits in the hub's 1024-record queue during a storage outage (up to about 17 min) keeps its real receive time. If storage stamped the time instead, the timestamp would be off by however long the record waited.
104-- **Message magic is `0xA6`** (proposed), distinct from the on-disk record's `0xA7`, so a record can never be misread as a message.
105-- **Truncated messages are rejected at `recv()`,** which must return exactly `GT_MSG_SIZE`, not in decode. Decode keeps its fixed 16 B array.
106-- **Range checks stay in the hub:** decode doesn't check that light ≤ 65535. That is the hub's `reading_invalid` check.
107-- **Deferred:** `src/light_sensor_main.c` (open → sample → health → send) waited until the IPC client existed. The client was completed on 2026-10-24, so it is now the next item (see *Not done yet*).
108-- **`hub.sock` is AF_UNIX `SOCK_SEQPACKET`** (decided 2026-10-24). The kernel keeps message boundaries, so each `send()` is one whole 16 B message and each `recv()` returns exactly one message. A short message shows up as a short `recv()`, which is where DESIGN.md rejects it, and there are no partial sends to track. `SOCK_STREAM` would need frame reassembly and resync code in the hub.
109-- **`hub_listen` stands in for the hub on the Pi** (decided 2026-10-24) until `gt_hub_accept` and the hub process exist. It's a small tool (`src/hub_listen_main.c`, `make hub_listen`) that prints each decoded message and flags counter gaps. With it, the kill -9 / kill -STOP fault tests can run now.
110-- **The client never blocks the sampler:** every socket call is non-blocking, and `send` uses `MSG_NOSIGNAL`, so a dead hub returns `EPIPE` instead of raising SIGPIPE, which would kill the sensor process.
111-- **Time is passed in** (`now_ms` on `gt_ipc_client_send` / `_pump`), as with the health tracker, so the backoff tests need no clock and no sleeps.
112-- **Events are returned, not logged:** the client returns `GT_IPC_EV_DISCONNECT` / `_RECONNECT` / `_OVERFLOW` bit flags, and the caller logs `ipc_disconnect` / `ipc_reconnect` / `ipc_overflow`, the same pattern as `gt_health_record`.
113-- **`gt_ipc_client_open` doesn't connect.** A hub that isn't up yet is not an error (DESIGN.md, Mechanism E: processes tolerate unavailable peers at startup). The first attempt happens on the first send or pump.
114-- **GAP needs 2 free slots:** after an overflow, a new reading is accepted only when the ring can hold both the GAP and the reading. This keeps the order: old data, then the GAP, then new data. Otherwise the reading is refused and counted into the same gap.
