int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
    /* Line-buffer stdout so each message line appears at once, even when
     * the output is piped to a log (e.g. `| tee hub.log`). */
    setvbuf(stdout, NULL, _IOLBF, 0);

    if (argc != 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "usage: %s /run/plantpi/hub.sock\n", argv[0]);
        return 2;
    }
    int lock_fd = take_instance_lock(argv[1]);
    if (lock_fd < 0) {
        return 1;
    }
    int listen_fd = bind_listener(argv[1]);
    if (listen_fd < 0) {
        close(lock_fd);
        return 1;
    }
    printf("hub_listen: listening on %s\n", argv[1]);

    struct pollfd conns[1 + MAX_SENSOR_CONNS];
    conns[0].fd = listen_fd;
    conns[0].events = POLLIN;
    size_t conn_count = 1;
    counter_track tracks[GT_SENSOR_SOIL + 1];
    memset(tracks, 0, sizeof tracks);

    for (;;) {
        if (poll(conns, (nfds_t)conn_count, -1) < 0) {
            if (errno == EINTR) {
                continue;
            }
            perror("hub_listen: poll");
            break;
        }

        /* Invariant: conns[1..conn_index-1] are open and served this round.
         * A closed connection is replaced by the last entry, which is then
         * checked in the same slot, so no entry is skipped. */
        size_t conn_index = 1;
        while (conn_index < conn_count) {
            if ((conns[conn_index].revents & (POLLIN | POLLHUP | POLLERR)) &&
                !serve_one(conns[conn_index].fd, tracks)) {
                close(conns[conn_index].fd);
                conns[conn_index] = conns[conn_count - 1];
                conn_count--;
                continue;
            }
            conn_index++;
        }

        /* Accept after serving, so a new entry's revents (still 0) is never read. */
        if (conns[0].revents & POLLIN) {
            int conn_fd = accept(listen_fd, NULL, NULL);
            if (conn_fd >= 0 && conn_count < 1 + MAX_SENSOR_CONNS) {
                conns[conn_count].fd = conn_fd;
                conns[conn_count].events = POLLIN;
                conns[conn_count].revents = 0;
                conn_count++;
                printf("sensor connected (fd %d)\n", conn_fd);
            } else if (conn_fd >= 0) {
                printf("too many sensor connections; refusing fd %d\n", conn_fd);
                close(conn_fd);
            }
        }
    }

    /* Invariant: conns[1..conn_index-1] have been closed. */
    for (size_t conn_index = 1; conn_index < conn_count; conn_index++) {
        close(conns[conn_index].fd);
    }
    close(listen_fd);
    unlink(argv[1]);
    close(lock_fd);   /* last: the socket file is removed while still locked */
    return 1;
}
8:- **Goal of the IPC phase (Steps 10–24):** a reading leaves the sensor process and reaches a hub stand-in (`hub_listen`) over `hub.sock`. It survives the hub being killed and restarted (buffered, then resent in order, or covered by a GAP), and the client never blocks the 1 Hz sampling.
98:- **Time is passed in** as `now_ms`, matching `gt_sampler_tick`, so tests need no clock.
102:- **Log sink deferred:** the formatter only fills a caller-provided buffer. Where lines go (stderr, a file or the monitor process) and reading the wall clock are decided with the light process. DESIGN.md doesn't specify either.
110:- **The client never blocks the sampler:** every socket call is non-blocking, and `send` uses `MSG_NOSIGNAL`, so a dead hub returns `EPIPE` instead of raising SIGPIPE, which would kill the sensor process.
111:- **Time is passed in** (`now_ms` on `gt_ipc_client_send` / `_pump`), as with the health tracker, so the backoff tests need no clock and no sleeps.
122:- **One listener per socket, enforced by a lock** (decided 2026-10-24). A process that listens on a socket (`hub_listen` now; the hub and storage later) first takes an `fcntl` write lock on `<socket>.lock` and holds it for its whole life. Only the lock holder may remove a leftover socket file and bind a new one; a second copy prints `already running` and exits without touching anything. The kernel drops the lock when the holder dies, `kill -9` included, so a crash never leaves a stale lock. This is safer than probing with `connect()`, which leaves a gap: two copies started together could both see the file as stale, and the second would take the first one's address. Closing *any* fd the process holds on the lock file releases the lock, so the file is opened in one place only. When the hub is written, this moves into a shared `src/` helper; testing it needs `fork()`, because a process never blocks on its own `fcntl` lock.
439:- **New `tests/test_ipc_loopback.c`:** a real `SOCK_SEQPACKET` socket in a `mkdtemp` directory under `/tmp` (Windows-mounted drives in WSL2 don't reliably support socket files). `now_ms` is passed in, so the tests never sleep. Nothing blocks either: the listener is `SOCK_NONBLOCK` and every `recv` uses `MSG_DONTWAIT`, so a missing message is a FAIL line, not a hang. A small fixture comes in with IPL-01: the `loopback` struct plus `loopback_init`, `make_listener`, `recv_frame` and `loopback_cleanup`.
460:- **New `src/hub_listen_main.c`:** `bind_listener`, `serve_one`, then `main`, plus `take_instance_lock` (see *Decisions*: one listener per socket).
463:  - **`bind_listener`:** removes a socket file left behind by a `kill -9`, but only if it is a socket (`lstat` + `S_ISSOCK`) and only while holding the instance lock.
475:- **`src/light_sensor_main.c`:** sampler at 1000 ms + health tracker + IPC client, plus a heartbeat to `sup.sock`. Also the log sink (stderr, a file or the monitor process) and wall-clock timestamps for log lines.
487:  - **All done (2026-10-24):** every file above. The `hub_listen` instance lock also added a Decision to this file (Step 23).
511:| 4 | The client survives hub failures | Pi: `kill -9 hub_listen` for 10 s, then for 90 s; `kill -STOP` for 60 s | 10 s: one `ipc_disconnect` + `ipc_reconnect`, about 10 buffered readings arrive in order, no gap. 90 s: `ipc_overflow`, then a GAP with `value` ≈ 26 (90 − 64), and the data counters + `gap.value` cover every counter. STOP: sends get `EAGAIN` while sampling holds 1 Hz with p99 jitter < 10 ms (the client never blocks) |
543:| 23 | `hub_listen` on-Pi tool + instance lock | ✅ Done (2026-10-24): `make hub_listen` builds; smoke-tested in WSL against the real client |
552:- **`src/light_sensor_main.c`** (not started, next): the 1000 ms sampler, the health tracker and the IPC client (`send` each reading, `pump` every tick), plus a heartbeat to `sup.sock`. This is also where the log sink (stderr, a file or the monitor process) and wall-clock timestamps for log lines get decided. Its prerequisite, the IPC client, is now done. It unblocks IPC ladder rungs 3–4 on the Pi against `hub_listen`.
553:- **`gt_hub_accept`** (no `src/hub.c` yet): its signature was updated in Step 7 to output a `gt_record`, but it isn't implemented. It has to validate the message (including the `reading_invalid` range check), stamp `seq` and the µs receive time, and build the record that `gt_record_encode` turns into bytes for `storage.sock`. The hub process that calls it replaces `hub_listen` (rung 6). It must follow the one-listener-per-socket lock rule (*Decisions*), and that is the point where the lock moves into a shared `src/` helper with `fork()`-based tests.
594:  - `hub_listen`, a `poll()`-based hub stand-in for the Pi, with a single-instance lock.
611:  - A `hub_listen` killed with `kill -9` leaves its socket file behind. Removing that file safely needed the one-listener-per-socket lock, now a *Decision* the hub and storage will follow too. Probing with `connect()` was rejected because two copies started together could both see the file as stale.

